comment_data.body:
@justinmk @zeertzjq thanks for the explanation!

If I reuse `AlignTextPos`, wouldn't that suggest that `stl_align` can handle "center alignment" (there's currently no syntax for that, but it could be invented), and that `get_bordertext_col`/`nvim_open_win` can handle "no alignment" for title/footer (which doesn't seem to make sense in that context)? `StlAlignment` seems less confusing to me.

Since `kAlignNone` actually means "don't fill up all the theoretically left-over space, unless there are `%=`s," which is useful for the flexible-width ruler that actually already is right-aligned for other reasons, maybe I should avoid the word alignment altogether? E.g.
```c
// Specifies whether/where to add padding to reach target width.
typedef enum {
  kPaddingNone,  // previously kAlignNone
  kPaddingLeft,  // previously kAlignRight
  kPaddingRight, // previously kAlignLeft
} StlPadding;
```
The only possible issue with this is the inversion of left and right, but I thinks it's clear enough. I couldn't find another padding enum to reuse.

An alternative that I considered in the beginning is to use something like `AlignTextPos` (or worse, leave the alignment implicit in the sign of `target_width`) and an additional boolean that controls whether to actually use the specified alignment or not. But I think it's weird if one argument can make another useless depending on the value, kind of "mutually exclusive" parameters (see `:h dev-api`)

BTW: maybe, I should pass this argument to `build_stl_str_hl`. `kPaddingNone` for `titlestring`/`ruler`/.. and `kPaddingRight` for `statusline`/`winbar`/.. Then the corresponding code that fills up the rest could be removed from `win_redr_stl_expr` and I guess somewhere in ui2.

